ORR (vector, immediate): implement the two ASIMDIMM forms.

`TryDecodeORR_ASIMDIMM_L_HL` and `_L_SL` were generated stubs returning false, so
every `ORR <Vd>.<T>, #<imm8>{, LSL #<amount>}` reached `_ecv_unreached`.

⭐ This is the first lifter target in this tree proven reachable by RUNNING
rather than by static inventory. Guest 0x87ab18 in `ruby-glibc.fused`, encoding
`0f04141c` = `orr v28.2s, #0x80`, inside `proc_options` immediately after a
`strncmp(arg, "yjit", 4)`: it is the vectorised
`FEATURE_SET(opt->features, FEATURE_BIT(yjit))`, so every `--yjit*` spelling
SIGILLs with `[BUG] Illegal instruction`, exit 127.

The implementation is BIC's, with `|` instead of `& ~`. That is not a shortcut:
`BIC (vector, immediate)` and `ORR (vector, immediate)` share an encoding shape,
an arrangement specifier, and an immediate construction, and differ only in the
operation applied. The decoders are therefore byte-identical to BIC's and only
DATAXFER.cpp distinguishes them -- which is also why the stubs were the whole of
what was missing.

Three files, following the convention the existing patches use:
  * Decode.cpp   -- drop the two stubs (an implemented decoder lives in Arch.cpp)
  * Arch.cpp     -- the two real decoders, beside BIC's
  * DATAXFER.cpp -- ORR_L_HL / ORR_L_SL semantics and the four ISELs

⚠️ This unblocks ruby's argv parsing ONLY, not YJIT. The second wall behind it is
an address budget -- a 128 MiB reservation into a 96 MiB window -- which no
decoder patch touches. Implementing this makes `ruby --yjit` reach a DIFFERENT
fatal error, which is progress in knowledge rather than in capability.

